A week ago 160 employers came to a small school in the Rocky Mountains. I'd bet a lot of those students have signed offers by now.

It's Thursday morning here in Whitefish. Fall is trying hard to make an appearance but the summer sun will keep winning for a few more days. I filmed this on my last day at Montana Tech and finally sat down with the photos and video. Always fun going back through them.

These companies weren't just looking. They were hiring and they move fast. Offers go out within days of the fair. The smart companies don't wait around, they lock up their talent while everybody else is still reading resumes.

So why come all the way to Butte? A mile high, 126 years old, not exactly easy to get to.

I spent my career at ExxonMobil, BP and GE and worked next to Tech grads the whole way. They show up ready. They don't wait to be told what to do. They take the initiative to go one step further. The companies know it.

I wasn't that student at first. Got my first D ever in Calc 1 as a freshman. Retook it, got an A, ended up student body president. The place changes you. It bends your mind to think through how to solve problems. Halfway through your four years it's impossible to see the world the same way.

This was my second year spending the fair with students. Resumes, LinkedIn profiles, handshakes, eye contact, how to actually talk to the person who might hire you. Getting students to figure out their personal story, how to tell it, and what they want in a career to keep the adventure going. I love it.

After filming I got in the car, grabbed a Pork Chop John's sandwich (loaded, of course, if you know Butte you know) and made it home just before 10.

Back on campus in two weeks for homecoming.

If your company wasn't at this career fair, it absolutely should be. Every year Career Services is scrambling to find more space for companies to set up. DM me and I'll connect you with the right people at Tech.

