Kenya is strengthening global partnerships to unlock greater investment in nature, climate resilience and ecosystem restoration. At the 81st Session of the United Nations General Assembly, I advanced practical collaborations that can translate Kenya’s natural capital into sustainable livelihoods, climate action and inclusive green economic opportunities.

During my engagements at the 81st Session of the United Nations General Assembly (UNGA 81), I held a bilateral meeting with Dr. Sebastian Troëng, Chief Executive Officer of Conservation International (CI), to strengthen the longstanding partnership between the Government of Kenya and Conservation International.

We explored opportunities to deepen collaboration in nature conservation, ecosystem restoration, climate resilience and mobilization of nature-based finance. 

Conservation International offered its global and African technical expertise to support Kenya in mobilizing nature finance at scale through carbon markets and climate finance, including deploying its technical and investment teams to structure nature-finance solutions.

We also discussed Kenya’s carbon markets, with emphasis on strengthening technical and institutional capacity and addressing critical technical gaps that can unlock greater investment in our high-value carbon ecosystems.

Our discussions further covered GEF-9 and Green Climate Fund concepts being developed with relevant Government ministries and partners, targeting protected areas, forest reserves, climate resilience, and ecosystems in Kenya’s arid, semi-arid and coastal regions.

I invited Conservation International to partner with Government during the upcoming Mazingira Day activities, which will be implemented nationwide under Kenya’s National Tree Growing and Ecosystem Restoration Programme, with particular focus on the Mt. Elgon ecosystem, a transboundary landscape, biodiversity-rich ecosystem and critical water tower.

This collaboration will strengthen restoration, monitoring, seedling mobilization, community engagement, livelihoods and protection of cultural heritage, reaffirming our shared commitment to community-led conservation, nature finance, climate resilience, sustainable livelihoods and inclusive green economic opportunities.

